Article 38
Forms of services for victims as referred to in Article 36 letter b, consist of:
a. services for women and children victims of acts of violence, including: 1. complaint, consultation and counseling services;
2. assistance services;
3. health services;
4. legal aid services; and
5. repatriation and social reintegration services.
b. special services for women victims of acts of domestic violence or human trafficking, including:
1. complaint services;
2. pick-up services;
3. medical and psychological referral services;
4. shelter services;
5. assistance and legal aid services; and 6. repatriation and social reintegration services.

CHAPTER VI
RIGHTS OF VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE
Article 27
Women and children victims of violence receive the following rights: a. the right to have their dignity and worth as human beings respected; b. the right to health and psychological recovery from the suffering experienced by the victim;
c. the right to determine their own decisions;
d. the right to obtain information;
e. the right to confidentiality;
f. the right to compensation;
g. the right to social rehabilitation;
h. the right to complaint handling;
i. the right to assistance and legal aid;
j. the right to obtain visum et repertum free of charge; and k. the right of victims and their families to obtain facilitation in the judicial process.

CHAPTER V
FORMS OF VIOLENCE AGAINST WOMEN AND CHILDREN
Article 25
Forms of violence against women and children, include: a. physical violence;
b. psychological violence;
c. sexual violence;
d. neglect;
e. exploitation; and/or
f. other violence.

Paragraph 6
Protection of Child Victims of Physical and/or Mental Violence and Children with Disabilities Article 23 (1) Special Protection for child victims of physical and/or mental violence and children with disabilities as referred to in Article 17 letter f is carried out through efforts:
a. dissemination and socialization of provisions of laws and regulations that protect children from becoming victims of violence;
b. obtaining equal treatment with other children to achieve social integration as fully as possible and individual development; and
c. fulfillment of special needs.
(2) Implementation of special protection for children who become victims of physical and/or mental violence and children with disabilities as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out in the form of: a. Prevention Services, which include:
1. making policies for fulfilling special needs for children with disabilities and children experiencing psychosocial disorders; 2. providing education guarantees for child victims of physical and/or mental violence and children with disabilities;
3. providing protection guarantees from all forms of violence, exploitation, neglect and maltreatment for children with disabilities;
4. providing socialization and raising awareness of parents and/or the community, press, educators, by the government and law enforcement officials about the adverse impacts of violence;
5. providing socialization and raising awareness of parents and/or the community, press, educators, by the government about protecting the identity of children who become victims of physical and/or mental violence and children with disabilities from negative stigma; and
6. establishing complaint mechanisms for case handling.
b. Risk Reduction Services, including:
1. conducting early identification, complaint services and assessment of children and families at risk;
2. providing services for children and families who are victims of violence which include:
a. guidance and counseling on parenting patterns and behavior change;
b. legal consultation;
c. mediation to related parties;
d. psychosocial services;
e. economic assistance and life skills;
f. temporary shelter; and
g. protecting the identity of victims.
c. Case Handling Services, which include:
1. conducting immediate rescue efforts for victims in conditions dangerous to their safety;
2. providing referrals according to the needs of victims;
3. conducting counseling and support from family;
4. treatment of children in a humane manner in accordance with the dignity and rights of children;
5. providing a sense of security and protection from news coverage to avoid negative stigma;
6. providing special facilities and infrastructure for children with disabilities;
7. providing rehabilitation and social reintegration services; and 8. creating a database of child victims of physical and/or mental violence and children with disabilities.

REGENT OF TASIKMALAYA
WEST JAVA PROVINCE
REGIONAL REGULATION OF TASIKMALAYA REGENCY
NUMBER 5 YEAR 2019
CONCERNING
WOMEN'S EMPOWERMENT AND CHILD PROTECTION
BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD
THE REGENT OF TASIKMALAYA,

Considering : a. that every citizen has equal and equivalent rights to live a dignified life in accordance with the principles of humanity, equality and justice;
b. that women and children are groups of society who are vulnerable to acts of injustice and inequality as well as acts of violence that can harm their rights and dignity as human beings;
c. that the rampant cases of violence against women and children in Tasikmalaya Regency require handling and concrete action from the Regional Government to eliminate all forms of violence against women and children;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b, and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning Women's Empowerment and Child Protection;

In View of : 1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 14 Year 1950 concerning the Establishment of Regency Areas within West Java Province (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1950), as amended by Law Number 4 Year 1968 concerning the Establishment of Purwakarta Regency and Subang Regency by amending Law Number 14 Year 1950 concerning the Establishment of Regency Areas within West Java Province (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1968 Number 31, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 2851);
3. Law Number 7 Year 1984 concerning Ratification of the Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1984 Number 29, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3277);
4. Law Number 39 Year 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
5. Law Number 23 Year 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235) as amended several times, most recently by Law Number 17 Year 2016 concerning Stipulation of Government Regulation in Lieu of Law Number 1 Year 2016 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 Year 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2016 Number 99, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5882);
6. Law Number 23 Year 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
7. Law Number 13 Year 2006 concerning Protection of Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2006 Number 64, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4635) as amended by Law Number 31 Year 2014 concerning Amendment to Law Number 13 Year 2006 concerning Protection of Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2014 Number 293, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5602);
8. Law Number 21 Year 2007 concerning Eradication of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2007 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);
9. Law Number 11 Year 2012 concerning the Juvenile Criminal Justice System (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2012 Number 153, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5332);
10. Law Number 23 Year 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as amended several times, most recently by Law Number 9 Year 2015 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 Year 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679);
11. Government Regulation Number 4 Year 2006 concerning Implementation and Cooperation for Recovery of Victims of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2006 Number 15, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4604);
12. Government Regulation Number 12 Year 2017 concerning Guidance and Supervision of Regional Government Administration (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2017 Number 73, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6041);
13. Regulation of the Minister of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection of the Republic of Indonesia Number 12 Year 2015 concerning Guidelines for Child Participation in Development Planning (State News of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2015 Number 1754);
14. Regional Regulation of Tasikmalaya Regency Number 7 Year 2016 concerning Establishment and Composition of Regional Apparatus (Regional Gazette of Tasikmalaya Regency Year 2016 Number 7 as amended by Regional Regulation of Tasikmalaya Regency Number 1 Year 2019 concerning Amendment to Regional Regulation of Tasikmalaya Regency Number 7 Year 2016 concerning Establishment and Composition of Regional Apparatus (Regional Gazette of Tasikmalaya Regency Year 2019 Number 1);

With Mutual Approval of THE REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF TASIKMALAYA REGENCY and

