FORMS OF VIOLENCE AGAINST WOMEN AND CHILDREN
Article 25
Forms of violence against women and children, include: a. physical violence;
b. psychological violence;
c. sexual violence;
d. neglect;
e. exploitation; and/or
f. other violence.

CHAPTER VI
RIGHTS OF VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE
Article 27
Women and children victims of violence receive the following rights: a. the right to have their dignity and worth as human beings respected; b. the right to health and psychological recovery from the suffering experienced by the victim;
c. the right to determine their own decisions;
d. the right to obtain information;
e. the right to confidentiality;
f. the right to compensation;
g. the right to social rehabilitation;
h. the right to complaint handling;
i. the right to assistance and legal aid;
j. the right to obtain visum et repertum free of charge; and k. the right of victims and their families to obtain facilitation in the judicial process.

Article 20
(1) Special protection for children who are economically and/or sexually exploited as referred to in Article 17 letter c, constitutes the obligation and responsibility of the Regional Government and society.
(2) The implementation of special protection for children who are economically and or sexually exploited as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out in the form of:
a. Prevention Services, including:
1) Creating policies for the protection of children from all forms of violence, exploitation, and maltreatment in all situations of children's lives; 2) Creating policies for the fulfillment of special needs for children with disabilities and children experiencing psychosocial disorders who are victims of economic and/or sexual exploitation;
3) Providing protection guarantees for child victims of economic and/or sexual exploitation;
4) Providing protection guarantees from all forms of violence, exploitation, neglect and maltreatment for children who are economically and/or sexually exploited;
5) Providing socialization and awareness raising for parents and/or society, the press, educators, by the government and law enforcement officials regarding the adverse impacts of violence and economic and/or sexual exploitation;
6) Providing socialization and awareness raising for parents and/or society, the press, educators, by the government and law enforcement officials regarding the protection of the identity of child victims of economic and/or sexual exploitation; and
7) Providing socialization regarding the provisions of laws and regulations related to the protection of children who are economically and/or sexually exploited.
b. Risk Reduction Services, which include:
1) Conducting early identification, complaint services and assessment of children and families at risk of experiencing economic and/or sexual exploitation;
2) Providing services for children and families who are victims of violence, and economic and/or sexual exploitation including:
a) legal consultation;
b) legal assistance to victims of sexual violence;
c) mediation to related parties;
d) psychosocial services;
e) economic assistance and life skills;
f) temporary shelter; and
g) protecting the identity of victims.
c. Case Handling Services, including:
1) Conducting immediate rescue efforts for victims who are in dangerous conditions for their safety;
2) Providing referrals according to the needs of victims;
3) Providing counseling and support from family; 4) Treating child victims of economic and/or sexual exploitation humanely in accordance with the dignity and rights of children; 5) Providing a sense of security and protection for child victims of economic and/or sexual exploitation from media coverage; and 6) Providing rehabilitation and social reintegration services.

