Article 21
(1) Supervision, prevention, care, and rehabilitation of children who become victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic substances, and other addictive substances (NAPZA) abuse as referred to in Article 17 letter d and are involved in their production and distribution, shall be the obligation and responsibility of the Regional Government and the community.
(2) The implementation of protection for children who become victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic substances, and other addictive substances (NAPZA) abuse as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be conducted in the form of:
a. Prevention Services, including:
1. Formulating policies for the protection of children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic substances and other addictive substances (NAPZA) abuse;
2. Formulating special protection policies for children who become victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic substances, and other addictive substances (NAPZA) abuse; and
3. Providing socialization and raising awareness of parents and/or the community and children about the adverse effects of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic substances and other addictive substances (NAPZA) abuse.
b. Risk Reduction Services, including:
1. Conducting early identification, complaint services and assessment of children and families at risk;
2. Providing services for children and families who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic substances, and other addictive substances (NAPZA) abuse including:
a. guidance and counseling on parenting patterns and behavior change;
b. legal consultation; and
c. psychosocial services.
c. Case Handling Services, including:
1. Conducting immediate rescue efforts for victims whose safety is in dangerous conditions;
2. Providing referrals according to the needs of victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic substances and other addictive substances (NAPZA) abuse;
3. Conducting counseling and ensuring support from the family;
4. Treating children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic substances, and other addictive substances (NAPZA) abuse in a humane manner in accordance with the dignity and rights of children; and
5. Providing rehabilitation and social reintegration services.

Article 22
(1) Special Protection for child victims of abduction, sale and trafficking as referred to in Article 17 letter e is carried out through supervision, protection, prevention, care and rehabilitation efforts by the Regional Government and community.
(2) The implementation of special protection for children who are victims of abduction, sale and trafficking as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out in the form of:
a. Prevention Services, including:
1. Providing protection guarantees for child victims of abduction, sale and trafficking; and
2. Providing complaint mechanisms and case handling. b. Risk Reduction Services, including:
1. Conducting early identification, complaint services and assessment of children and families at risk; and
2. Providing services for children and victims of abduction, sale and trafficking which include:
a. guidance and counseling on parenting patterns and behavior change;
b. legal consultation;
c. mediation to related parties;
d. psychosocial services;
e. economic assistance and life skills;
f. temporary shelter; and
g. protecting the identity of victims.
c. Case Handling Services, including:
1. Conducting immediate rescue efforts for victims who are in dangerous conditions for their safety;
2. Providing referrals according to the needs of victims;
3. Conducting counseling and having support from family; 4. Treating child victims of abduction, sale and trafficking in a humane manner in accordance with the dignity and rights of children;
5. Providing accessibility to obtain information regarding case developments;
6. Providing rehabilitation and social reintegration services; and 7. Creating a database of child victims of abduction, sale and trafficking.

Article 16
The implementation of child protection in the social sector as referred to in Article 10 letter e shall be carried out in the form of:
a. Prevention services, including:
1) Creating policies on child protection from all forms of violence, exploitation, neglect and maltreatment in all aspects of children's lives;
2) Creating policies on social welfare guarantees for children who experience violence, exploitation, neglect and maltreatment; and
3) Organizing training to increase capacity for family welfare consultation institutions.
b. Risk Reduction Services, including:
1) Conducting early identification, complaint services and assessment of children and families who are victims of violence, exploitation, neglect and maltreatment;
2) Providing services for children and families who are victims of violence, exploitation, neglect and maltreatment including:
1) guidance and counseling on parenting patterns and behavior change; 2) mediation to related parties;
3) psychosocial services;
4) economic assistance and life skills;
5) temporary shelter;
6) preparation of social reintegration for child victims of violence, exploitation, neglect and maltreatment; and
7) protecting the identity of victims to prevent negative stigma for children.
c. Case Handling Services, including:
1) Conducting immediate rescue efforts for victims who are in dangerous conditions for their safety;
2) Providing referrals according to the needs of victims;
3) Providing counseling and support from families; 4) Providing rehabilitation and social reintegration services;
5) Conducting monitoring of the condition of children in alternative care in the long term; and
6) Creating a database on alternative care facilities for children.

